INT. DENTAL CAR - MOBILE CLINIC TRAIN - NIGHT

A converted railway carriage fitted with a reclining dental chair, a tray of instruments, and a hanging lamp. Rails clatter below. A window frames the black countryside. Through it, scattered farm lights wink by.

DR. PERPETUA ACHTERBERG-NWOSU, 50s, in surgical mask and visor, leans over a patient in the chair. Her hygienist, MARISOL TEAGUE-ROY, 30s, holds the suction. In the corner, the patient's mother, a WOMAN in a gray coat, clutches a purse. The patient, a small boy named DESMOND, 7, grips the armrests, eyes squeezed shut.

DR. ACHTERBERG
Open wide for me, Desmond.

DESMOND
(muffled)
Is it going to hurt?

DR. ACHTERBERG
It's going to pinch. Pinch is not hurt.

DESMOND
It's hurt with a nicer name.

DR. ACHTERBERG
You're very smart.

MARISOL
Suction ready.

DR. ACHTERBERG
Thank you. She says it's ready, you hear that?

DESMOND
I heard.

The train rounds a bend. The instrument tray slides and Marisol catches it with one hand.

WOMAN
Is it a cavity?

DR. ACHTERBERG
Two. And a baby tooth that's refusing to leave.

WOMAN
My husband had one like that.

DR. ACHTERBERG
Refusing to leave?

WOMAN
Don't get me started.

MARISOL
Doctor, she's slid a note under the door. The conductor's.

DR. ACHTERBERG
Tell her I'm elbow-deep in a seven-year-old.

MARISOL
It says it's urgent.

DR. ACHTERBERG
It's always urgent. Hold his jaw.

MARISOL
Holding.

Dr. Achterberg crosses to the door, picks the note off the floor, and reads it. She returns with her mask pulled down.

DR. ACHTERBERG (CONT'D)
Marisol, finish up. Mrs. Okonkwo-Bell, I'll need you in the next carriage.

WOMAN
Why?

DR. ACHTERBERG
The note says your husband is on this train.

A silence. The boy opens one eye.

DESMOND
Mom? Is Dad here?

MRS. OKONKWO-BELL
(very still)
No.

DR. ACHTERBERG
Then we have a problem.

MRS. OKONKWO-BELL
Doctor, I haven't seen him in three years.

DR. ACHTERBERG
He bought a ticket in your name.

MRS. OKONKWO-BELL
That's impossible.

Marisol removes the suction. Desmond sits up. The woman in the gray coat stands, clutching her purse, and faces the door.
